---
description: Rules for TypeScript and JavaScript
globs: "**/*.{js,ts,mjs,cjs}"
alwaysApply: false
---

# Control flow
- Always use curly braces on `if`, `else`, `for`, `while`, and `do` bodies (enforced by ESLint `curly`)
- Prefer `if` over `switch`

# Error handling
- Always throw an Error
- Never catch errors silently

# Formatting
- Follow `Prettier` (`prettier.config.js`) and `ESLint` (`.eslintrc.json`) rules
- Use single quotes wherever possible
- Prefer template literals to string addition.  E.g. prefer `Please go to ${destination}` over `'Please go to ' + destination`

# Functions
- Prefer arrow function expressions to hoisted function declarations
    - Bad: `function foo(): void {}`
    - Good: `const foo = (): void => {};`
- Always add type annotations to parameters and return types
- If there are more than two parameters, use a destructured object parameter
- Always use parentheses around the return value when an arrow function returns an object without using `{}`.  E.g. `const foo = (): object => ({ bar: 'baz' });`

# Naming
- Use long, pronounceable variable names.  E.g. use `submissionResponse` instead of `subRes`
    - Exception: It is OK to use `el` in a short arrow function.  e.g. `submissionArray.map((el) => el.id)`
- Use kebab-case for filenames
- Use a mix of kebab-case and periods for test filenames, e.g. `title-filter.ts`, `title-filter.spec.ts`
- Use explanatory variables, not magic expressions


# Operators
- Never use `delete`

# Test files
- Test files should be named the same as the file to be tested with the extension `.spec.ts` (or `.test.ts` where the project already uses that)
- Test files should be in the same folder as the file to be tested

# Types and Interfaces
- Prefer `type` over `interface` in most cases to prevent accidental declaration merging
- You MUST use `type` for unions, intersections, primitives, and tuples
- Use `interface` ONLY for the following scenarios:
    - Extending the global environment (e.g., `Window`)
    - Writing a library where we expect end users to extend the interface
    - Increasing compile-time performance on projects where compile time exceeds 90 seconds
    - Defining massive, deeply-nested object structures
- Avoid adding types and interfaces in the same file as implementation code. Move them to a separate file
    - Exception: test files can co-locate types with tests
    - Exception: frontend (Nuxt/Vue) may co-locate component-local types in the same file
